  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule, and the rule is enabled and running. I haven't checked that the alert's search actually finds the 5xx requests. I tried to count them with a direct query, but Kibana rejected it with a \"missing authentication credentials\" error.\n\n**1. Fields in the data.** Each record in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request, with a `timestamp` time field. The main fields are:\n- **What was requested:** `request` and `url`.\n- **The result:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text) and `bytes`.\n- **Who asked:** `clientip`, `agent` and `machine.os`.\n- **Where from:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`.\n- **Other:** `extension`, `host`, `referer` and `tags`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-65eda4d5 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-65eda4d5-web-logs-overview-c93e07c54d93`, set to the last 7 days). It has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Traffic by source country (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-65eda4d5 5xx spike\"** (id `fe3521bb-859c-4bda-8582-3dd0ef71075f`). It runs every minute and searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` for responses whose code starts with \"5\". It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes, measured on the `timestamp` field. Its status is \"pending\", which just means it hasn't finished its first run yet.\n\nThe alert relies on the status codes being stored as three-character text like \"503\". If they're stored some other way, the rule will never fire. Its first run should show whether the search matches anything.",
